Realizing single crystals with multiple components directly from solution becomes increasingly difficult as the number of building blocks is raised. This is because the self-assembly process requires a collection of simultaneous interactions to be manipulated in a cooperative manner. When this fails, alternative products are able to form. In the case of MOFs, this is fatal since the purification of crystalline products is virtually impossible.To date, the number of discrete molecules that can be co-crystallized and occupy inequivalent positions in a molecular solid is just five[5] reflecting the difficulty of the challenge. To add further complexity, compared with molecular solids, constructing multicomponent MOFs in one pot requires accommodating all the components into a specific and preferred architecture with high openness and porosity that is consistent with a particular network topology. Previously, we have increased the structural complexity of multicomponent MOFs by producing quaternary MOFs from one metal and three linkers[3d], and from two metals and two linkers[6]. Recently, two examples of MOFs with one metal and four organic linkers were reported by Zhang[7] and Zhou[8], in which three distinctive linkers were sequentially installed into Zr-based MOFs in a postsynthetic fashion. Such postsynthetic methods offer a complementary approach to bottom-up self-assembly. However, two-step processes introduce mass transfer barriers and tend to place a limitation on the uniformity of the final material since vacant sites may remain. Incorporating five distinct components into a well-ordered MOF in a straightforward one-step process, equaling the record in molecular solids, remains a pressing challenge.
Pushing the envelope for multicomponent MOFs, single crystals of a five-component (quinary) MOF, named FDM-8,were herein synthesized by a solvothermal reaction of two metal salts $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $Z n(N O_{3})_{2})$ and three kinds of linear organic linkers (4-pyrazolecarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P y C})$ ，1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ ，and 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{N D C});$ (see the Supporting Information). Single-crystal $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (SXRD) revealed that FDM-8 crystalizes in the tetragonal space group $P4_{2}/m m c_{;}$ with $a=b=28.{\overset{\cdot}{10}}\mathbb{A}$ and $c=25.86\bar{\mathsf{A}}$ (Table S1). As shown in Figure 1, two kinds of inorganic secondary building units (SBUs)[9] are present in the structure: triangular $C u_{3}^{1}(N N)_{3}$ motifs involving the $\mathsf{P y C}$ linkers and octahedral $Z\mathsf{n}^{\mathsf{I I}}4\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}$ clusters involving all three organic linkers. These two SBUs are further connected by three linear linkers with different lengths to generate a (3,6)-coordinated network with an ith-d topology. In contrast to other MOF structures with ith-d topology, in which preformed organictris(carboxylates)[3c3d3e,10]such as1,35-trcarboxyphenyl)benzene $(H_{3}B T B)$ were usually employed, FDM-8 features a metalloligand that self-assembles from PyC and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ under the solvothermal reaction conditions. This metalloligand, referred to as ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}^{\mathsf{I}}({\mathsf{P y C}})_{3}$ ，can also be viewed as a threeconnected SBU node joined by three linkers. Underlying the selfassembly of FDM-8 at the expense of other competing products (vide infra), the differential binding affinities between the two metal ions and the donor groups on the organic linkers allow the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ ions to exclusively coordinate to the pyrazolate nitrogen donors and the $Z n(11)$ ions to solely bind to the carboxylate oxygens. These self-sorting characteristics based on well-established principles of coordination chemistry are the key to forming two kinds of SBUs with different connecting geometries in a one-pot assembly process. A
One of the underlying principles of the reticular chemistry of frameworks is that MOFs are constructed and crystallized simultaneously. Based on one kind of coordination interaction involving a complementary set of building blocks (one metal and one linker),most MOFs are binary structures. Given the five synthons present in the solvothermal reaction mixture that produces FDM-8, a large number of framework possibilities exist that, intuitively,would appear to be unavoidable. Indeed,more than 70 MOF structures have been reported based on only two of the five aforementioned constituents (Zn, Cu, PyC,BDC,and NDC) (Table S4). These MOFs, along with conceivable structures with more than two components[6.11i, are all possible competing phases given the building blocks available in the synthesis mixture. Remarkably, however, the exclusive assembly of FDM-8 in a phase-pure form was observed. This was validated by powder
X-ray diffraction (PXRD, Figure S3), in which the diffraction peaks are coincident with simulated positions based on the single crystal structure. The bulk purity of FDM-8 was further supported by a $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum of the crystals after acid digestion and Cu removal in which the molar ratio of the three organic linkers $(\mathsf{P y C}{\cdot}\mathsf{B D C}{\cdot}\mathsf{N D C}={\sim}12.0{:}2.10{:}1.05$ ，Figure S43) corresponds to the formula of FDM-8 $[(\mathsf{Z n_{4}O})_{3}(\mathsf{C u_{3}}(\mathsf{P y C})_{3})_{4}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}(\mathsf{N D C})]$ In addition, the molar ratio of the two metals $(Z\mathsf{n}\colon\mathsf{C u})$ is 0.99:1.00, matching with the theoretical value of 1:1. Sharing the same $C u_{3}^{1}(N N)_{3}$ SBU with the previously reported FDM-3-7,[6] the triangular unit could be oxidized to $\mathsf{C u}^{\mathsf{I I}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{3}(\mu\mathsf{-O H})(\mathsf{N N})_{3}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ while maintaining its node connectivity, and converted back to $\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathsf{I}}(\mathsf{N N})_{3}$ upon activation at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in vacuo (see Section 7 in the Supporting Information for detailed studies on the reversible redox activity).[6.12] The reversible oxidation state switch of the constituent Cu ions does not compromise the framework integrity, as demonstrated by PXRD and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (Figures S1, S3, and S46-S52). To the best of our knowledge, FDM-8 is the first quinary MOF structure synthesized in a one-pot bottom-up self-assembly process. The constitution of this remarkable material is driven by a lattice topology that supports the preferred outcome of a complex set of complementary equilibria involving four synchronized metal-donor pairs: Zn(ll) with carboxylates from BDC，NDC，and PyC，and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ with pyrazolates from PyC.
Placing this result in the broader context of multicomponent MOFs, we note that the one-step construction of frameworks with multiple components in a regular and ordered way have relied on two main strategies: (i) The diversified node geometry strategy (Figure 2a),which was demonstrated in the synthesis of the prototypical ternary MOF, UMCM-1[13]. In addition to employing just one SBU and one linear linker, as in the case of MOF-5[14], the centroid of a second triangular linker BTB in UMCM-1 defines a three-connected node resulting in a network with two kinds of node geometries. (ii) The diversified edge length strategy (Figure 2a), which led to the successful synthesis of the first quaternary MOF,MUF- $\cdot7a^{[3\mathrm{d}]}$ . This structure is achieved by placing two kinds of linear linkers, BDC and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate (BPDC), with different lengths into the structure, and thus creating a
multiplicity of edges in the underlying network. In this work,we have added a third layer of complexity that operates in conjunction with these two strategies. Here, the triangular organic linker is deconstructed into the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathsf{I}}(\mathsf{N N})_{3}$ -based metal-ligand complex in situ with a geometry and metrical parameters that remain mutually compatible with the other linkers. This boosts the number of components of the network beyond that observed for quaternary MOFs. Combining the deconstruction strategy (Figure 2a) with the diversified node geometry and edge length approaches, FDM8 advances our understanding of design strategies on the evolution route (Figure 2b) towards complex - yet well-ordered - MOFs.
As revealed by SXRD,two metals and three linkers with ordered spatial arrangements generate in FDM-8 two kinds of microporous cages and one kind of mesoporous cage that interconnect in three-dimensional space (Figure 2a). The two microporous cages 8l and 8ll (pink and green spheres in Figure 2a) are built by four $Z\mathsf{n}^{\prime\prime}{}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}$ and four $\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathsf{I}}(\mathsf{N N})_{3}$ clusters as nodes,which are further connected by eight PyC, and two BDC (in cage 8l) or two NDC (in cage 8ll) linkers. The cages 8l and 8ll possess slightly different sizes $(7.{\dot{6}}\times9.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and ${\bar{6}}.2\times11.2{\mathbb{A}}.$ respectively). On the other hand, the mesoporous cage 8ll (yellow sphere in Figure 2a) is dodecahedral in shape,with an inner size of $22.4\times24.6\mathbb{\ A}$ . To experimentally investigate the porosity of FDM-8, the as-synthesized FDM-8 crystals were washedwith $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and solventexchanged with $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ , followed by activation at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in vacuo. After solvent removal (Figure S53), the solid retained its structure, as validated by PXRD (Figure S3). The permanent porosity of the activated FDM-8 was assessed by Ar and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at 87 and $77~\mathsf{K}$ (Figures 3a and S19), respectively. Calculated using the Ar isotherm, FDM-8 has an impressive Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $3643~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $1.543\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . In the associated pore distribution profile (Figures 3b, S13, and S14), both micropores (1.05-1.65 nm in diameter) and mesopores $(2.05{-}2.25\mathsf{n m}$ in diameter) were observed, corresponding to the cages 8l and 8ll, and the cage 8Ill! respectively. The BET surface area of FDM-8 based on its ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherm at $77\mathsf{K}$ is $3905~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S19).
Recent studies show that finely-tuned hierarchical porosity of this type is useful in achieving high capacity for methane storage at high pressure.[15] The methane adsorption isotherm of FDM-8 up to 80 bar was measured using a high-pressure volumetric gas adsorption analyzer at various temperatures between 273 and $308\mathsf{K}$ (Figures S21-S24). As shown in Figure 3c, FDM-8 delivers a total volumetric capacity of $215c m^{3}\sin c m^{-3}$ at 80 bar at $298\mathsf{K}$ This total volumetric capacity increases to $257~\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{s t p}~\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ at 80 bar when the measurement was conducted at $273\ \mathsf{K}$ The corresponding uptake value is $204\ c m^{3}\mathsf{s t p}\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ at $308\mathsf{K}$ The nearzero coverage isosteric heat of adsorption $(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}})$ is calculated to be 10.4 kJ mol-1 based on the isotherms at various temperatures (Figures S39 and S42, Table S6),which is comparable to those of most carboxylate and azolate MOFs, but lower than
$\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I})$ -based MOFs with open metal sites.[16]  The $\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}}$ slightly increases at high pressure, which is probably due to $C H_{4}-C H_{4}$ interactions at high gas loading.[3g.1od,15b,17]Considering the retention of a minimum inlet pressure of 5 bar, the methane volumetric capacity above this pressure, termed the working capacity, is more meaningful in practical operation settings.[10c] FDM-8 has an uptake of only $22c m^{3}\sin c m^{-3}$ at 5 bar at $298~\mathsf{K},$ which makes the working capacity of this material $193\ c m^{3}\sin$ cm' 3 at $5\substack{-80}$ bar. This value places FDM-8 among one of the best known materials for high pressure methane storage. The reticular chemistry of frameworks with ith-d topology is frequently observed to lead to both micropores and mesopores,[3c,3d,3e,6,10,18] and thus underlies higher methane storage capacities. Among all these ith-d MOFs (Table S5) $[3\mathsf{c},3\mathsf{d},10\mathsf{c},10\mathsf{d}]$ theworkingcapacity of FDM-8 nearly rivals that of the best reported MOF, MOF-905 (203 $\mathsf{c m}_{\mathsf{s t p}}^{3}\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ at $298~\mathsf{K})$ [10c]  which is  constructed with  more sophisticated acrylate linkers. Overall, the hierarchical porosity that arises from the straightforward assembly process using only short and simple linkers endows FDM-8 with outstanding porosity with high pressure methane storage capacity.
With its precise arrangement of three kinds of organic linkers encompassing three kinds of interconnected pores, FDM-8 provides an ideal platform for systematically preparing isoreticular analogs by placing functional groups on the linkers prior to the assembly of the lattice. In this way, the pore space defined by the framework can be systematically programmed. This additional layer of complexity elevates multicomponent MOFs towards sophisticated biomolecules such as enzymes, which feature compartmentalized spatial arrays of specific functional groups. This strategy was implemented in FDM-8 as illustrated in Figure 4a. It is worth noting that the cage 8l does not involve NDC, and BDC is not present in the cage 8ll, providing the opportunity of specifically functionalizing one micropore without altering the chemical environment of the other micropore. On the other hand, the mesoporous cage 8lll features all three linkers on the surface, adding a degree of control to its size, topology, and chemical characteristics. In addition, the two SBUs containing two different kinds of metals introduce the prospect of functionalizing the framework with different inorganic elements, which will generate diverse functionalities.
To realize this experimentally in FDM-8, we chose two derivatives of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ with differing electronic characteristics: 2- aminoterephthalic acid $(N H_{2}-H_{2}B D C)$ and 1,4- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid $(C_{4}H_{4}\mathrm{-}H_{2}B D C)$ . These functionalized linkers replaced ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ in the one-pot synthesis and the FDM-8 framework itself. The newly introduced groups may influence interactions between the five constituents during the crystallization processes; however,with careful control over the ratio of the molecular precursors, we converged on synthetic methods to deliver two functionalized quinary MOFs, namely $N H_{2^{-}}$ FDM-8 (Figure 4b） and $C_{4}H_{4}$ -FDM-8 (Figure 4c), respectively, with perfect phase purity. $N H_{2}$ -FDM-8 and $C_{4}H_{4}$ -FDM-8 share the stoichiometry and lattice structure of FDM-8 as validated by
To investigate the mechanical stability of these quinary MOFs, thermally activated FDM-8, $N H_{2}$ -FDM-8,and $C_{4}H_{4}$ -FDM- $\cdot8$ were pressed into pellets at ${\sim}1$ MPa (Figures S1 and S2). The consistency between the PXRD patterns of the pelletized MOFs and the simulated patterns indicated all three MOFs remained highly crystalline after pelletizing (Figure S12). Calculated from the low pressure ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms, the BET surface areas of pelletized FDM-8, $N H_{2}$ -FDM-8, and $C_{4}H_{4}$ -FDM-8 are 3613,3295, and $2895\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S20). Furthermore, these pelletized MOFs show excess methane uptakes of $231$ ,230,and $209\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{s t p}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, at 80 bar at $298\mathsf{K}$ (Figures S36-S38). These values are only $9.0\%$ ， $2.9\%,$ and $5.0\%$ lower than the counterparts in single crystal form, confirming their good mechanical stability. FDM-8, $N H_{2}$ -FDM-8,and $C_{4}H_{4}$ -FDM-8after immersed in water for 24 hours show new XRD patterns (Figure S5), indicating possible structural transformations due to coordination bond dissociations. However， evidenced by the PXRD,theseMOFs are stable in DMF $N H_{2}O$ mixtures (volume ratio of 9:1) for one week (Figures S6-S8), and in the air for at least 24 hours (Figures S9-S11).
In summary, we have harnessed bottom-up self-assembly to position five distinct components in single crystalline FDM-8. By combining a deconstruction strategy with the diversified node geometry and edge length approaches, two metals and three linkers are positioned in an orderly way throughout the framework, to set a new record for the complexity of self-assembled multicomponent MOFs. With hierarchical pores,FDM-8 shows high porosity with a BET surface area of $3643~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and an impressive methane storage working capacity of $193c m^{3}\mathrm{stp}\ c m^{-3}$ at $5\substack{-80}$ bar at $298~\mathsf{K}$ . Furthermore, systematic programming of chemical constituents and pore environments has been demonstrated by the construction of two exemplary isoreticular structures of FDM-8. With compartmentalized spatial arrays of both specific organic functional groups and distinct metal sites, these porous frameworks provide rational platforms for tailormade functions.
